0 0 0 fC
225 227 1888 (             Similarities exist between the BDOS functions and the BIOS functions, particularly for) 1888 SB
225 286 1903 (simple device I/O.  For example, when a transient program makes a console output function call) 1903 SB
225 345 1804 (to the BDOS, the BDOS makes a console output call to the BIOS.  In the case of disk I/O,) 1804 SB
225 405 1857 (however, this relationship is more complex.        The BDOS might make many BIOS function) 1857 SB
225 464 1939 (calls to perform a single BDOS file I/O function.     BDOS disk I/O is in terms of 128-byte logical) 1939 SB
225 523 1300 (records.  BIOS disk I/O is in terms of physical sectors and tracks.) 1300 SB
225 641 1846 (             The System Control Block \(SCB\) is a 100-byte, decimal, CP/M 3 data structure that) 1846 SB
225 700 1947 (resides in the BDOS system component.  The BDOS and the BIOS communicate through fields in) 1947 SB
225 760 1770 (the SCB.  The SCB contains BDOS flags and data, CCP flags and data, and other system) 1770 SB
225 819 1915 (information, such as console characteristics and the current date and time.  You can access some) 1915 SB
225 878 1009 (of the System Control Block fields from the BIOS.) 1009 SB
225 996 1899 (             Note that the SCB contains critical system parameters which reflect the current state of) 1899 SB
225 1055 1898 (the operating system.  If a program modif i es these parameters, the operating system can crash.) 1898 SB
225 1115 1770 (See Section 3 of this manual, and the description of BDOS Function 49 in the CP/M Plus) 1770 SB
225 1174 1441 (Proqrammer's Guide for more information on the System Control Block.) 1441 SB
225 1292 1944 (             Page Zero is a region of memory that acts as an interface between transient programs and) 1944 SB
225 1351 1906 (the operating system.  Page Zero contains critical system parameters, including   the entry to the) 1906 SB
225 1410 1914 (BDOS and the entry to the BIOS Warm BOOT routine.  At system start-up, the BIOS initializes) 1914 SB
225 1470 1909 (these two entry points in Page Zero.   All linkage between transient programs and the BDOS   is) 1909 SB
225 1529 1887 (restricted to the indirect linkage through Page Zero.  Figure  1-1 illustrates the general memory) 1887 SB
225 1588 484 (organization of CP/M 3.) 484 SB
1188 2890 25 (3) 25 SB
