942 345 516 (CP/M 3 BIOS Overview) 516 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 582 1943 (              This section describes the organization of the CP/M 3 BIOS and the BDOS jump vector.) 1943 SB
225 641 1765 (It provides an overview of the System Control Block, followed by a discussion of system) 1765 SB
225 700 1903 (initialization procedures, character I/O, clock support, disk I/O, and memory selects and moves.) 1903 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c133 font
225 819 755 (          2.1 Organization of the BIOS) 755 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 937 1933 (              The BIOS is the CP/M 3 module that contains all hardware- dependent input and output) 1933 SB
225 996 1836 (routines.  To configure CP/M 3 for a particular hardware environment, use the sample BIOS) 1836 SB
225 1055 1709 (supplied with this document and adapt it to the specific hardware of the target system.) 1709 SB
225 1174 1849 (              Alternatively, you can modify an existing CP/M 2.2 BIOS to install CP/M 3 on your) 1849 SB
225 1233 1793 (target machine.  Note that an unmodified CP/M 2.2 BIOS does not work with the CP/M 3) 1793 SB
225 1292 1899 (operating system.  See Appendix C for a description of the modifications necessary to convert a) 1899 SB
225 1351 721 (CP/M 2.2 BIOS to a CP/M 3 BIOS.) 721 SB
225 1470 1926 (               The BIOS is a set of routines that performs system initialization, character-oriented I/O) 1926 SB
225 1529 1866 (to the console and printer devices, and physical sector I/O to the disk devices.  The BIOS also) 1866 SB
225 1588 1895 (contains routines that manage block moves and memory selects for systems with bank-switched) 1895 SB
225 1647 1898 (memory.  The BIOS supplies tables that define the layout of the disk devices and allocate buffer) 1898 SB
225 1706 1929 (space which the BDOS uses to perform record blocking and deblocking.  The BIOS can maintain) 1929 SB
225 1765 1079 (the system time and date in the System Control Block.) 1079 SB
225 1884 1908 (               Table 2-1 describes the entry points into the BIOS from the Cold Start Loader and the) 1908 SB
225 1943 1780 (BDOS.  Entry to the BIOS is through a jump vector.  The jump vector is a set of 33 jump) 1780 SB
225 2002 1469 (instructions that pass program control to the individual BIOS subroutines.) 1469 SB
225 2120 1775 (               You must include all of the entry points in the BIOS jump vector in your BIOS.) 1775 SB
225 2180 1886 (However, if your system does not support some of the functions provided for in the BIOS, you) 1886 SB
225 2239 1882 (can use empty subroutines for those functions.  For example, if your system does not support a) 1882 SB
225 2298 1862 (printer, JMP LIST can reference a subroutine consisting of only a RET instruction.  Table 2-1) 1862 SB
225 2357 776 (shows the elements of the jump vector.) 776 SB
1175 2890 50 (15) 50 SB
