225 1470 1866 (          The BOOT and WBOOT routine must load the CCP into the TPA in Bank I at location) 1866 SB
225 1529 1885 (0100H.   The CCP can be loaded in two ways.  If there is sufficient space on the system tracks,) 1885 SB
225 1588 1946 (the CCP can be stored on the system tracks and loaded from there.  If you prefer, or if there is not) 1946 SB
225 1647 1799 (sufficient space on the system tracks, the BIOS Cold BOOT routine can read the CCP into) 1799 SB
225 1706 821 (memory from the file CCP.COM on disk.) 821 SB
225 1825 1949 (          If the CCP is in a COM file, use the BOOT and WBOOT routines to perform any necessary) 1949 SB
225 1884 1896 (system initialization, then use the BDOS functions to OPEN and READ the CCP.COM file into) 1896 SB
225 1943 1677 (the TPA.  In bank- switched systems, the CCP must be read into the TPA in Bank 1.) 1677 SB
225 2061 1868 (          In bank-switched systems, your Cold BOOT routine can place a copy of the CCP into a) 1868 SB
225 2120 1924 (reserved area of an alternate bank after loading the CCP into the TPA in Bank 1. Then the Warm) 1924 SB
225 2180 1851 (BOOT routine can copy the CCP into the TPA in Bank 1 from the alternate bank, rather than) 1851 SB
225 2239 1592 (reloading the CCP from disk, thus avoiding all disk accesses during warm starts.) 1592 SB
225 2357 1926 (          There is a 128-byte buffer in the resident portion of the BDOS in a banked system that can) 1926 SB
225 2416 1763 (be used by BOOT and WBOOT.  The address of this buffer is stored in the SCB variable) 1763 SB
225 2475 1827 (@BNKBF.  BOOT and WBOOT can use this buffer when copying the CCP to and from the) 1827 SB
225 2535 292 (alternate bank.) 292 SB
225 2653 1844 (          The system tracks for CP/M 3 are usually partitioned as shown in the following figure;) 1844 SB
