225 227 1885 (          Note that you can define a single peripheral as the LIST, AUXOUT, and AUXIN device) 1885 SB
225 286 1890 (simultaneously.   If you assign no peripheral device as the LIST, AUXOUT, or AUXIN device,) 1890 SB
225 345 1914 (the AUXOUT and LIST routines can just return, and the AUXIN routine can return with a 1AH) 1914 SB
225 405 1197 (\(CTRL-Z\) in register A to indicate an immediate end-of-file.) 1197 SB
225 523 1938 (          CP/M 3 supports character device I/O redirection.  This means that you can direct a logical) 1938 SB
225 582 1841 (device, such as CONIN or AUXOUT, to one or more physical devices.  The DEVICE utility) 1841 SB
225 641 1871 (allows you to reassign devices and display, and to change the current device configurations, as) 1871 SB
225 700 1943 (described in the CP/M Plus User's Guide.  The I/O redirection facility is optional.  You should not) 1943 SB
225 760 1159 (implement it until the rest of your BIOS is fully functional.) 1159 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c133 font
225 878 309 (    2.5 Disk I/O) 309 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 996 1922 (          The BDOS accomplishes disk I/O by making a sequence of calls to the various disk access) 1922 SB
225 1055 1909 (subroutines in the BIOS.  The subroutines set up the disk number to access, the track and sector) 1909 SB
225 1115 1924 (on a particular disk, and the Direct Memory Access \(DMA\) address and bank involved in the I/O) 1924 SB
225 1174 1939 (operation.  After these parameters are established, the BDOS calls the READ or WRITE function) 1939 SB
225 1233 713 (to perform the actual I/O operation.) 713 SB
225 1351 1950 (          Note that the BDOS can make a single call to SELDSK to select a disk drive, follow it with) 1950 SB
225 1410 1790 (a number of read or write operations to the selected disk, and then select another drive for) 1790 SB
225 1470 454 (subsequent operations.) 454 SB
225 1588 1927 (          CP/M 3 supports multiple sector read or write operations to optimize rotational latency on) 1927 SB
225 1647 1921 (block disk transfers.  You can implement the multiple sector I/O facility in the BIOS by using the) 1921 SB
225 1706 1949 (multisector count passed to the MULTIO entry point.  The BDOS calls MULTIO to read or write) 1949 SB
225 1765 1897 (up to 128 sectors.  For every sector number 1 to n, the BDOS calls SETDMA then calls READ) 1897 SB
225 1825 227 (or WRITE.) 227 SB
225 1943 1813 (          Table 2-5 shows the sequence of BIOS calls that the BDOS makes to read or write a) 1813 SB
225 2002 1911 (physical disk sector in a nonbanked and a banked system.  Table 2-6 shows the sequence of calls) 1911 SB
225 2061 1738 (the BDOS makes to the BIOS to read or write multiple contiguous physical sectors in a) 1738 SB
225 2120 616 (nonbanked and banked system.) 616 SB
