225 1410 1808 (              The CP/M 3 BDOS performs its own blocking and deblocking of logical 128-byte) 1808 SB
225 1470 1918 (records.  Unlike earlier versions of CP/M, the BIOS READ and WRITE routines always transfer) 1918 SB
225 1529 1886 (physical sectors as specified in the Disk Parameter Block to or from the DMA buffer. The Disk) 1886 SB
225 1588 1887 (Parameter Header defines one or more physical sector buffers which the BDOS uses for logical) 1887 SB
225 1647 639 (record blocking and deblocking.) 639 SB
225 1765 1939 (              In a banked environment, CP/M 3 maintains a cache of deblocking buf fers and directory) 1939 SB
225 1825 1901 (records using a Least Recently Used \(LRU\) buffering scheme.  The LRU buffer is the first to be) 1901 SB
225 1884 1918 (reused when the system runs out of buffer space.  The BDOS maintains separate buffer pools for) 1918 SB
225 1943 679 (directory and data record caching.) 679 SB
225 2061 1907 (              The BIOS contains the data structures to control the data and directory buffers and the) 1907 SB
225 2120 1844 (hash tables.  You can either assign these buffers and tables yourself in the BIOS, or allow the) 1844 SB
225 2180 973 (GENCPM utility to generate them automatically.) 973 SB
225 2298 1782 (              Hash tables greatly speed directory searching.  The BDOS can use hash tables to) 1782 SB
225 2357 1819 (determine the location of directory entries and therefore reduce the number of disk accesses) 1819 SB
225 2416 1907 (required to read a directory entry.  The hash table allows the BDOS to directly access the sector) 1907 SB
225 2475 1790 (of the directory containing the desired directory entry without having to read the directory) 1790 SB
225 2535 1918 (sequentially.  By eliminating a sequential read of the directory records, hashing also increases the) 1918 SB
225 2594 1856 (percentage of time that the desired directory record is in a buffer, eliminating the need for any) 1856 SB
225 2653 1826 (physical disk accesses in these cases. Hash tables and directory caches eliminate many of the) 1826 SB
225 2712 1871 (directory accesses required when accessing large files.  However, in a nonbanked system, hash) 1871 SB
225 2771 939 (tables increase the size of the operating system.) 939 SB
