225 227 1904 (          When the BIOS finds an error condition, the READ and WRITE routines should perform) 1904 SB
225 286 1885 (several retries before reporting the error condition to the BDOS.  Ten retries are typical.  If the) 1885 SB
225 345 1830 (BIOS returns an error condition to the BDOS, the BDOS reports the error to the user in the) 1830 SB
225 405 310 (following form:) 310 SB
225 523 709 (             CP/M Error on d: Disk I/O) 709 SB
225 641 1282 (     The d: represents the drive specification of the relevant drive.) 1282 SB
225 760 1877 (          To provide better diagnostic capabilities for the user, it is often desirable to print a more) 1877 SB
225 819 1843 (explicit error message from the BIOS READ or WRITE routines before the BIOS returns an) 1843 SB
225 878 1764 (error code to the BDOS.  The BIOS should interrogate the SCB Error Mode Variable to) 1764 SB
225 937 1249 (determine if it is appropriate to print a message on the console.) 1249 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c133 font
225 1055 725 (     2.6 Memory Selects and Moves) 725 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 1174 1846 (          Four BIOS functions are provided to perform memory management. The functions are) 1846 SB
225 1233 1919 (MOVE, XMOVE, SELMEM, and SETBNK.  The XMOVE, SELMEM, and SETBNK memory) 1919 SB
225 1292 1352 (management routines are applicable to the BIOS of banked systems.) 1352 SB
225 1410 1922 (          The BDOS uses the BIOS MOVE routine to perform memory-to-memory block transfers.) 1922 SB
225 1470 1884 (In a banked system, the BDOS calls XMOVE to specify the source and destination banks to be) 1884 SB
225 1529 1863 (used by the MOVE routine. If you use memory that is not in the common area for data record) 1863 SB
225 1588 1002 (buffers, you must implement the XMOVE routine.) 1002 SB
225 1706 1861 (          The BDOS uses SELMEM when the operating system needs to execute code or access) 1861 SB
225 1765 905 (data in other than the currently selected bank.) 905 SB
225 1884 1757 (          The BDOS calls the SETBNK routine prior to calling disk READ or disk WRITE) 1757 SB
225 1943 1815 (functions.  The SETBNK routine must save its specified bank as the DMA bank.  When the) 1815 SB
225 2002 1934 (BDOS invokes a disk I/O routine, the I/O routine should save the current bank number and select) 1934 SB
225 2061 1807 (the DMA bank prior to the disk READ or WRITE.  After completion of the disk READ or) 1807 SB
225 2120 1910 (WRITE, the disk I/O routine must reselect the current bank. Note that when the BDOS calls the) 1910 SB
225 2180 975 (disk I/O routines, Bank 0 is in context \(selected\).) 975 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c133 font
225 2298 452 (     2.7 Clock Support) 452 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 2416 1847 (          If the system  has a real-time clock or is capable of keeping time, possibly by  counting) 1847 SB
225 2475 1875 (interrupts from a counter/timer chip, then the BIOS can maintain the time of day in the System) 1875 SB
225 2535 1902 (Control Block and update the time on clock interrupts.  BIOS Function 26 is provided for those) 1902 SB
225 2594 1161 (systems where the clock is unable to generate an interrupt.) 1161 SB
