225 464 344 (            XLT       ) 344 SB
675 464 1479 (Set the XLT field to the address of the logical to hysical sector translation ) 1479 SB
675 523 1485 (table.  If there is no sector translation and the    logical and physical sector ) 1485 SB
525 582 1366 (numbers are the same, set XLT to 0000H. Disk drives with identical ) 1366 SB
525 641 1112 (sector skew factors can share the same translation table.) 1112 SB
225 760 286 (                      ) 286 SB
675 760 1309 (XLT is the value passed to SECTRN in registers DE. Usually the ) 1309 SB
675 819 1443 (translation table consists of one byte per physical sector.  Generally, it is ) 1443 SB
675 878 1395 (advisable to keep the number of physical sectors per logical track to a ) 1395 SB
675 937 1490 (reasonable value to prevent the translation table from becoming too large.  ) 1490 SB
525 996 1337 (In the case of disks with multiple heads, you can compute the head ) 1337 SB
375 1055 1218 (number from the track address rather than the sector address.) 1218 SB
225 1174 306 (            -0-       ) 306 SB
675 1174 1430 (These 72 bits \(9 bytes\) of zeroes are the scratch area the BDOS uses to ) 1430 SB
675 1233 1074 (maintain various parameters associated with the drive.) 1074 SB
225 1351 331 (            MF        ) 331 SB
675 1351 1403 (MF is the Media Flag. The BDOS resets MF to zero when the drive is ) 1403 SB
675 1410 1477 (logged in.  The BIOS can set this flag and @MEDIA in the SCB to 0FFH ) 1477 SB
675 1470 1457 (if it detects that a drive door has been opened.  If the flag is set to 0FFH, ) 1457 SB
675 1529 1471 (the BDOS checks for a media change prior to performing the next BDOS ) 1471 SB
675 1588 1323 (file operation on that drive. If the BDOS determines that the drive ) 1323 SB
675 1647 1399 (contains a new volume, the BDOS performs a login on that drive, and ) 1399 SB
675 1706 1387 (resets the MF flag to 00H.  Note that the BDOS checks this flag only ) 1387 SB
675 1765 1481 (when a system call is made, and not during an operation. Usually, this flag ) 1481 SB
525 1825 1152 (is used only by systems that support door-open interrupts.) 1152 SB
225 1943 344 (            DPB       ) 344 SB
675 1943 1488 (Set the DPB f ield to the address of a Disk Parameter Block that describes ) 1488 SB
675 2002 1467 (the characteristics of the disk drive.  Several Disk Parameter Headers can ) 1467 SB
675 2061 1425 (address the same Disk Parameter Block if their drive characteristics are ) 1425 SB
675 2120 1350 (identical.  \(The Disk Parameter Block is described in Section 3.3.3.\)) 1350 SB
