675 464 1487 (CSV is the address of a scratchpad area used to detect changed disks.        ) 1487 SB
675 523 1453 (This address must be different for each removable media Disk Parameter ) 1453 SB
675 582 1389 (Header.  There must be one byte for every 4 directory entries \(or 128 ) 1389 SB
675 641 1386 (bytes of directory\).  In other words, length\(CSV\) = \(DRM/4\)+l. \(See ) 1386 SB
675 700 1234 (Table 3-3 for an explanation of the DRM field.\) If the drive is ) 1234 SB
675 760 1465 (permanently mounted, set the CKS variable in the DPB to 8000H and set ) 1465 SB
675 819 1459 (CSV to 0000H.  This way, no storage is reserved for a checksum vector. ) 1459 SB
675 878 1431 (The checksum vector may be located in common memory or in Bank 0. ) 1431 SB
675 937 1328 (Set CSV to 0FFFEH for GENCPM to set up the checksum vector.) 1328 SB
225 1055 309 (         ALV       ) 309 SB
675 1055 1404 (ALV is the address of the scratchpad area called the allocation vector, ) 1404 SB
675 1115 1403 (which the BDOS uses to keep disk storage allocation information.       ) 1403 SB
675 1174 796 (This area must be unique for each drive.) 796 SB
225 1292 247 (                   ) 247 SB
675 1292 1477 (The allocation vector usually requires 2 bits for each block on the drive.    ) 1477 SB
675 1351 1479 (Thus, length\(ALV\) = \(DSM/4\) + 2.   \(See Table 3-3 for an explanation of ) 1479 SB
675 1410 1453 (the DSM field.\) In the nonbanked version of CP/M 3, you can optionally ) 1453 SB
675 1470 1409 (specify that GENCPM reserve only one bit in the allocation vector per ) 1409 SB
675 1529 1179 (block on the drive.  In this case, length\(ALV\) = \(DSM/8\) +) 1179 SB
225 1647 247 (                   ) 247 SB
675 1647 1426 (The GENCPM option to use single-bit allocation vectors is provided in ) 1426 SB
675 1706 1458 (the nonbanked version of CP/M 3 because additional memory is required ) 1458 SB
675 1765 1418 (by the double-bit allocation vector.  This option applies to all drives on ) 1418 SB
675 1825 222 (the system.) 222 SB
225 1943 247 (                   ) 247 SB
675 1943 1442 (With double-bit allocation vectors, CP/M 3 automatically frees, at every ) 1442 SB
675 2002 1470 (system warm start, all file blocks that are not permanently recorded in the ) 1470 SB
675 2061 1361 (directory.  Note that file space allocated to a f ile is not permanently ) 1361 SB
675 2120 1458 (recorded in a directory unless the file is closed.  Therefore, the allocation ) 1458 SB
675 2180 1455 (vectors in memory can indicate that space is allocated although directory ) 1455 SB
675 2239 1487 (records indicate that space is free for allocation.  With single-bit allocation ) 1487 SB
525 2298 1327 (vectors, CP/M 3 requires that a drive be      reset before this space ) 1327 SB
1875 2298 153 ( can be ) 153 SB
525 2357 1489 (reclaimed.  Because it increases performance, CP/M 3 does not reset disks ) 1489 SB
375 2416 1438 (at system warm start.  Thus, with single-bit allocation vectors, if you do ) 1438 SB
375 2475 1348 (not reset the disk system, DIR and SHOW can report an inaccurate ) 1348 SB
225 2535 746 (amount of free space.  With single-bit) 746 SB
1175 2890 50 (38) 50 SB
