225 227 391 (         DTBL Macro) 391 SB
225 345 1934 (              Use the DTBL macro to generate the drive table, DRVTBL.  It has one parameter, a list) 1934 SB
225 405 1323 (of the DPHs in your system.  The list is enclosed in angle brackets.) 1323 SB
225 523 900 (              The form of the DTBL macro call is) 900 SB
225 582 1091 (                  label: DTBL      <DPHA,DPHB,...,DPHP>) 1091 SB
225 700 1945 (where DPHA is the address of the DPH for drive A, DPHB is the address of the DPH for drive B,) 1945 SB
225 760 544 (up to drive P. For example,) 544 SB
225 878 1269 (                 DRVTBL: DTBL       <ACSHDO,FDSDO,FDSD1>) 1269 SB
225 996 1890 (         This example generates the drive table for a three-drive system. The DTBL macro always) 1890 SB
225 1055 1904 (generates a sixteen-word table, even if you supply fewer DPH names.      The unused entries are) 1904 SB
225 1115 1194 (set to zero to indicate the corresponding drives do not exist.) 1194 SB
225 1233 361 (         DPH Macro) 361 SB
225 1351 1856 (              The DPH macro routine generates a Disk Parameter Header \(DPH\) . It requires two) 1856 SB
225 1410 1867 (parameters: the address of the skew table for this drive, and the address of the Disk Parameter) 1867 SB
225 1470 1908 (Block \(DPB\) .    Two parameters are optional:    the maximum size of the checksum vector, and) 1908 SB
225 1529 1935 (the maximum size of the allocation vector.  If you omit the maximum size of the checksum vector) 1935 SB
225 1588 1949 (and the maximum size of the allocation vector from the DPH macro invocation, the corresponding) 1949 SB
225 1647 1935 (fields of the Disk Parameter Header are set to OFFFEH so that GENCPM automatically allocates) 1935 SB
225 1706 232 (the vectors.) 232 SB
225 1825 883 (               The form of the DPH macro call is) 883 SB

