975 582 108 (None) 108 SB
225 700 1891 (             To transfer logically consecutive disk sectors to or from contiguous memory locations,) 1891 SB
225 760 1933 (the BDOS issues a MULTIO call, followed by a series of READ or WRITE calls. This allows the) 1933 SB
225 819 1868 (BIOS to transfer multiple sectors in a single disk operation.  The maximum value of the sector) 1868 SB
225 878 1922 (count is dependent on the physical sector size, ranging from 128 with 128-byte sectors, to 4 with) 1922 SB
225 937 1848 (4096-byte sectors. Thus, the BIOS can transfer up to 16K directly to or from the TPA with a) 1848 SB
225 996 329 (single operation.) 329 SB
225 1115 1906 (             The BIOS can directly transfer all of the specified sectors to or from the DMA buffer in) 1906 SB
225 1174 1529 (one operation and then count down the remaining calls to READ or WRITE.) 1529 SB
225 1292 1879 (             If the disk format uses a skew table to minimize rotational latency when single records) 1879 SB
225 1351 1950 (are transferred, it is more difficult to optimize transfer time for multisector transfers.        One way) 1950 SB
225 1410 1870 (of utilizing the multisector count with a skewed disk format is to place the sector numbers and) 1870 SB
225 1470 1925 (associated DMA addresses into a table until either the residual multisector count reaches zero, or) 1925 SB
225 1529 1936 (the track number changes.  Then you can sort the saved requests by physical sector to allow all of) 1936 SB
225 1588 1937 (the required sectors on the track to be read in one rotation.  Each sector must be transferred to or) 1937 SB
225 1647 602 (from its proper DMA address.) 602 SB
225 1765 1836 (             When an error occurs during a multisector transfer, you can either reset the multiple) 1836 SB
225 1825 1944 (sector counters in the BIOS and return the error immediately, or you can save the error status and) 1944 SB
225 1884 1658 (return it to the BDOS on the last READ or WRITE call of the MULTIO operation.) 1658 SB
