225 227 1347 (          3 \)BIOSKRNL calls the ?INIT entry of the BOOT module to) 1347 SB
225 286 1142 (             initialize other system hardware, such as memory) 1142 SB
225 345 1225 (             controllers, interrupts, and clocks.  It prints a sign-on) 1225 SB
225 405 994 (             message specific to the system, if desired.) 994 SB
225 523 1492 (          4\) BIOSKRNL calls ?LDCCP in the BOOT module to load the CCP) 1492 SB
225 582 440 (             into the TPA.) 440 SB
225 700 1461 (          5\) The BIOSKRNL module sets up Page Zero of the TPA with the) 1461 SB
225 760 1306 (             appropriate jump vectors, and passes control to the CCP.) 1306 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c133 font
225 878 713 (     4.3.2 Character I/O Operation) 713 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 996 1906 (          The CHARIO module performs all physical character I/O.  This module contains both the) 1906 SB
225 1055 1884 (character device table \(@CTBL\) and the routines for character input, output, initialization, and) 1884 SB
225 1115 1857 (status polling.  The character device table, @CTBL, contains the ASCII name of each device,) 1857 SB
225 1174 1220 (mode information, and the current baud rate of serial devices.) 1220 SB
225 1292 1879 (          To support logical to physical redirection of character devices, CP/M 3 supplies a 16-bit) 1879 SB
225 1351 1886 (assignment vector for each logical device.   The bits in these vectors correspond to the physical) 1886 SB
225 1410 1933 (devices.  The character I/O interface routines in BIOSKRNL handle all device assignment, calling) 1933 SB
225 1470 1918 (the appropriate character I/O routines with the correct device number.  The BIOSKRNL module) 1918 SB
225 1529 1486 (also handles XON/XOFF processing on output devices where it is enabled.) 1486 SB
225 1647 1934 (          You can use the DEVICE utility to assign several physical devices to a logical device.  The) 1934 SB
225 1706 1877 (BIOSKRNL root module polls the assigned physical devices, and either reads a character from) 1877 SB
225 1765 1851 (the first ready input device that is selected, or sends the character to all of the selected output) 1851 SB
225 1825 598 (devices as they become ready.) 598 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c133 font
225 1943 592 (     4.3.3 Disk I/O Operation) 592 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 2061 1834 (          The BIOSKRNL module handles all BIOS calls associated with disk I/O.  It initializes) 1834 SB
225 2120 1814 (global variables with the parameters for each operation, then invokes the READ or WRITE) 1814 SB
225 2180 1865 (routine for a particular controller.   The SELDSK routine in the BIOSKRNL calls the LOGIN) 1865 SB
225 2239 1904 (routine for a controller when the BDOS initiates a drive login. This allows disk density or media) 1904 SB
225 2298 723 (type to be automatically determined.) 723 SB
225 2416 1875 (          The DRVTBL module contains the sixteen-word drive table, @DTBL. The order of the) 1875 SB
225 2475 1877 (entries in @DTBL determines the logical to physical drive assignment.  Each word in @DTBL) 1877 SB
225 2535 1864 (contains the address of a DPH, which is part of an XDPH, as shown in Table 4-10.  The word) 1864 SB
225 2594 1788 (contains a zero if the drive does not exist.  The XDPH contains the addresses of the INIT,) 1788 SB
225 2653 1913 (LOGIN, READ, and WRITE entry points of the I/O driver for a particular controller.  When the) 1913 SB
225 2712 1796 (actual drivers are called, globally accessible variables contain the various parameters of the) 1796 SB
225 2771 778 (operation, such as the track and sector.) 778 SB
1175 2890 50 (74) 50 SB
