225 227 361 (        4.7 Disk I/O) 361 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 345 1887 (              The separation of the disk I/O section of the BIOS into several modules allows you to) 1887 SB
225 405 1926 (support each particular disk controller independently from the rest of the system. A manufacturer) 1926 SB
225 464 1884 (can supply the code for a controller in object module form, and you can link it into any existing) 1884 SB
225 523 1247 (modular BIOS to function with other controllers in the system.) 1247 SB
225 641 1893 (              The data structure called the Extended Disk Parameter Header, or XDPH, contains all) 1893 SB
225 700 1950 (the necessary information about a disk drive. BIOSKRNL.ASM locates the XDPH for a particular) 1950 SB
225 760 1902 (logical drive using the Drive Table.    The XDPH contains the addresses of the READ, WRITE,) 1902 SB
225 819 1946 (initialization, and login routines.  The XDPH also contains the relative unit number of the drive on) 1946 SB
225 878 1842 (the controller, the current media type, and the Disk Parameter Header \(DPH\) that the BDOS) 1842 SB
225 937 1436 (requires.  Section 3 of this manual describes the Disk Parameter Header.) 1436 SB
225 1055 1865 (              The code to read and write from a particular drive is independent of the actual CP/M) 1865 SB
225 1115 1890 (logical drive assignment, and works with the relative unit number of the drive on the controller.) 1890 SB
225 1174 1812 (The position of the XDPH entry in the DRVTBL determines the actual CP/M 3 drive code.) 1812 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c133 font
225 1292 616 (        4.7.1 Disk I/O Structure) 616 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 1410 1895 (              The BIOS requires a DRVTBL module to locate the disk driver. it also requires a disk) 1895 SB
225 1470 885 (module for each controller that is supported.) 885 SB
225 1588 1919 (              The drive table module, DRVTBL, contains the addresses of each XDPH defined in the) 1919 SB
225 1647 1919 (system.  Each XDPH referenced in the DRVTBL must be declared external to link the table with) 1919 SB
225 1706 478 (the actual disk modules.) 478 SB
225 1825 1903 (              The XDPHs are the only public entry points in the disk I/O modules.  The root module) 1903 SB
225 1884 1773 (references the XDPHs to locate the actual I/O driver code to perform sector READS and) 1773 SB
225 1943 1813 (WRITES.  When the READ and WRITE routines are called, the parameters controlling the) 1813 SB
225 2002 1902 (READ or WRITE operation are contained in a series of global variables that are declared public) 1902 SB
225 2061 393 (in the root module. ) 393 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c133 font
225 2180 902 (        4.7.2 Drive Table Module \(DRVTBL\)) 902 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 2298 1884 (              The drive table module, DRVTBL, def ines the CP/M absolute drive codes associated) 1884 SB
225 2357 459 (with the physical disks.) 459 SB
225 2475 1904 (              The DRVTBL module contains one public label, @DTBL. @DTBL is a 16-word table) 1904 SB
225 2535 1949 (containing the addresses of up to 16 XDPH'S.  Each XDPH name must be declared external in the) 1949 SB
225 2594 1914 (DRVTBL.  The first entry corresponds to drive A, and the last to drive P. You must set an entry) 1914 SB
225 2653 1777 (to 0 if the corresponding drive is undefined.  Selecting an undefined drive causes a BDOS) 1777 SB
225 2712 309 (SELECT error.) 309 SB
1175 2890 50 (81) 50 SB
225 92 248 (4.7 Disk I/O) 248 SB
1728 92 447 (CP/M 3 System Guide) 447 SB
