1071 227 257 (Appendix A) 257 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c133 font
839 345 721 (Removable Media Considerations) 721 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
225 582 1798 (               All disk drives under CP/M 3 are classified as either permanent or removable.  In) 1798 SB
225 641 1737 (general, removable drives support media changes; permanent drives do not.  Setting the) 1737 SB
225 700 1845 (high-order  bit in the CKS field in a drive's Disk Parameter Block \(DPB\) marks the drive as a) 1845 SB
225 760 334 (permanent drive.) 334 SB
225 878 1846 (               The BDOS file system distinguishes between permanent and removable drives.  If a) 1846 SB
225 937 1919 (drive is permanent, the BDOS always accepts the contents of physical record buffers as valid.  In) 1919 SB
225 996 1389 (addition, it also accepts the results of hash table searches on the drive.) 1389 SB
225 1115 1777 (               On removable drives, the status of physical record buffers is more  complicated.) 1777 SB
225 1174 1811 (Because of the potential for media change, the BDOS must discard directory buffers before) 1811 SB
225 1233 1815 (performing most directory related BDOS function calls. This is required because the BDOS) 1815 SB
225 1292 1930 (detects media changes by reading directory records.  When it reads a directory record, the BDOS) 1930 SB
225 1351 1927 (computes a checksum for the record, and compares the checksum to the currently stored value in) 1927 SB
225 1410 1824 (the drive's checksum vector .   If the checksum values do not match, the BDOS assumes the) 1824 SB
225 1470 1817 (media has changed.  Thus, the BDOS can only detect a media change by an actual directory) 1817 SB
225 1529 350 (READ operation.) 350 SB
225 1647 1861 (               A similar situation occurs with directory hashing on removable drives.   Because the) 1861 SB
225 1706 1923 (directory hash table is a memory-resident table, the BDOS must verify all unsuccessful hash table) 1923 SB
225 1765 1133 (searches on removable  drives by accessing the directory.) 1133 SB
225 1884 1775 (               The net result of these actions is that there is a significant performance penalty  ) 1775 SB
225 1943 1883 (associated with removable drives as compared to permanent drives.  In addition, the protection) 1883 SB
225 2002 1942 (provided by classifying a drive  as removable is not total.  Media changes are only detected during) 1942 SB
225 2061 1887 ( directory operations. If the media is changed on a drive during  BDOS WRITE operations, the) 1887 SB
225 2120 520 (new disk can be damaged.) 520 SB
225 2239 1798 (               The BIOS media flag facility gives you another option for supporting drives with) 1798 SB
225 2298 1921 (removable media.  However, to use this option, the disk controller must be capable of generating) 1921 SB
225 2357 1830 (an interrupt when the drive door is opened.  If your hardware provides this support, you can) 1830 SB
225 2416 1665 (improve the handling of removable media by implementing the following procedure:) 1665 SB
225 2535 250 (               1\) ) 250 SB
525 2535 1628 (Mark the drive as a permanent drive and set the DPB CKS parameter to th'e total ) 1628 SB
525 2594 1600 (number of directory entries, divided by four.  For example, set the CKS field for ) 1600 SB
525 2653 831 (a disk with 96 directory entries to 8018H.) 831 SB
1156 2890 88 (10 7) 88 SB
EJ RS
