32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c126 font
0 0 0 fC
225 227 185 (          2\) ) 185 SB
525 227 1612 (Implement an interrupt service routine that sets the @MEDIA flag in the System ) 1612 SB
525 286 1543 (Control Block and the DPH MEDIA byte for the drive that signaled the door ) 1543 SB
525 345 309 (open condition.) 309 SB
225 464 1805 (          By using the media flag facility, you gain the performance advantage associated with) 1805 SB
225 523 1945 (permanent drives on drives that support removable media.  The BDOS checks the System Control) 1945 SB
225 582 1870 (Block @MEDIA flag on entry for all disk-related function calls.  If the flag has not been set, it) 1870 SB
225 641 1875 (implies that no disks on the system have been changed.  If the flag is set, the BDOS checks the) 1875 SB
225 700 1928 (DPH MEDIA flag of each currently logged-in disk.  If the DPH MEDIA flag of a drive is set, the) 1928 SB
225 760 1843 (BDOS reads the entire directory on the drive to determine whether the drive has had a media) 1843 SB
225 819 1916 (change before performing any other operations on the drive.  In addition, it temporarily classifies) 1916 SB
225 878 1782 (any permanent disk with the DPH MEDIA flag set as a removable drive. Thus, the BDOS) 1782 SB
225 937 1871 (discards all directory physical record buffers when a drive door is opened to force all directory) 1871 SB
225 996 730 (READ operations to access the disk.) 730 SB
225 1115 1882 (          To summarize, using the BIOS MEDIA flag with removable drives offers two important) 1882 SB
225 1174 1758 (benefits.  First, because a removable drive can be classified as permanent, performance is) 1758 SB
225 1233 1935 (enhanced.  Second, because the BDOS immediately checks the entire directory before performing) 1935 SB
225 1292 1809 (any disk-related function an the drive if the drive's DPH MEDIA flag is set, disk integrity is) 1809 SB
225 1351 198 (enhanced.) 198 SB
